      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we need to find the critical points by taking the partial derivatives with respect to \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), and setting them to zero. This will help us determine the values of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) that minimize \\( z \\).\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivative of \\( z \\) with respect to \\( x \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = 2x + 6\n\\]\nSetting this equal to zero gives:\n\\[\n2x + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -3\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative of \\( z \\) with respect to \\( y \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 4y - 4\n\\]\nSetting this equal to zero gives:\n\\[\n4y - 4 = 0 \\implies y = 1\n\\]\n\nNow we have the critical point \\( (x, y) = (-3, 1) \\). To confirm that this point gives a minimum, we use the second derivative test. We compute the second partial derivatives:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial x^2} = 2, \\quad \\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial y^2} = 4, \\quad \\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial x \\partial y} = 0\n\\]\nThe discriminant \\( D \\) is given by:\n\\[\nD = \\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial x^2} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial y^2} - \\left( \\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial x \\partial y} \\right)^2 = 2 \\cdot 4 - 0^2 = 8\n\\]\nSince \\( D > 0 \\) and \\( \\frac{\\partial^2 z}{\\partial x^2} > 0 \\), the point \\( (x, y) = (-3, 1) \\) is a local minimum.\n\nFinally, we evaluate \\( z \\) at this point:\n\\[\nz = (-3)^2 + 2(1)^2 + 6(-3) - 4(1) + 22 = 9 + 2 - 18 - 4 + 22 = 11\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
