      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we can complete the square for the terms involving \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nFirst, let's complete the square for the \\( x \\) terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x \\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( x \\), which is 6, divide by 2 to get 3, and then square it to get 9. So we can write:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\nNext, let's complete the square for the \\( y \\) terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y \\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( y \\), which is -4, divide by 2 to get -2, and then square it to get 4. So we can write:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original function:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\]\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\nSince \\((x + 3)^2 \\geq 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 \\geq 0\\) for all real numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), the minimum value of \\( z \\) occurs when both \\((x + 3)^2 = 0\\) and \\(2(y - 1)^2 = 0\\). This happens when \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\).\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[ z = ( -3 + 3 )^2 + 2 ( 1 - 1 )^2 + 11 \\]\n\\[ z = 0 + 2 \\cdot 0 + 11 \\]\n\\[ z = 11 \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
